# Signed modular-symbol evaluation and Hecke eigenclasses

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.signed_evaluation` (`c502911d-8cbe-45cc-b1b9-0a803205f538`), by
davidloeffler, 2026-09-06.

Proof: submission `ea4f7568-fff1-4c3c-99d5-cf8ad756f253`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-07 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

The two signed projections of the integration class satisfy the explicit prime-Hecke, nebentype and
reflection eigenconditions. Integral coefficient evaluation recovers binomial(k−2,j) times the
signed integral, including the factor 1/2 and the polynomial reflection (−1)^j. The evaluation
functionals are defined over Z and extend coefficientwise.

## Explanation of the source proof

We construct the signed eigenpackets from the class $\Phi=I(f)$ of the eigenform $f$ and its
reflection. The submission assumes the sibling `integral_class_character_law` (the nebentype law for
$\Phi$) and uses the proved `reflection_class` (the reflection of a class is a class, commutes with
the prime Hecke operators, and preserves the nebentype law) and `reflection_involutive`.

Let $\psi$ be the class with $\psi=\mathcal R\Phi$ (`reflection_class`), and for a sign $s$ put
$$\phi_s=\tfrac12\big(\Phi+s\,\psi\big),\qquad s=\pm1 .$$

**Signed class.** The evaluation of $\psi$ at $(\infty,r)$ is the $X^jY^{n-j}$-coefficient of
$\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)\cdot\Phi(\infty,-r)$, since $\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)$ fixes $\infty$
and sends $r$ to $-r$ on cusps. The coefficient action of $\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)$ multiplies
$X^jY^{n-j}$ by $(-1)^j$ (`act_refl_monomial`; a homogeneous polynomial is the sum of its
$X^jY^{n-j}$ parts, `Sym_as_sum`), so by the integral-class property
$\operatorname{ev}_{j,r}(\psi)=(-1)^j\binom nj\,2\pi\int_0^\infty f(-r+it)(-r+it)^j\,dt$ (`hevψ`).
Hence
$$\operatorname{ev}_{j,r}(\phi_s)=\binom nj\cdot\frac{m_j(r)+s(-1)^jm_j(-r)}{2}=\binom
nj\,\operatorname{signedIntegral}(f,s,j,r),$$
which is `SignedClass`.

**Packet.** (i) Hecke: by Hecke-equivariance of $I$ and the eigen-equation $T_\ell f=a_\ell f$
(which needs no Hecke cusp-form lemma, since $a_\ell f$ is already a cusp form),
$T_\ell\Phi=a_\ell\Phi$; since $\mathcal R$ commutes with $T_\ell$ on classes (`reflection_class`),
also $T_\ell\mathcal R\Phi=a_\ell\mathcal R\Phi$; and $T_\ell$ is linear on cochains
(`primeHecke_add`, `primeHecke_smul`). (ii) Nebentype law: for $\Phi$ this is
`integral_class_character_law`, for $\mathcal R\Phi$ it is transported by `reflection_class`, and
the law is linear. (iii) Sign: $\mathcal R\phi_s=\tfrac12(\mathcal R\Phi+s\,\Phi)=s\,\phi_s$ because
$\mathcal R^2=1$ and $s^2=1$.

